== Chapter 13

#align(center)[THE UNITY OF THE DIVINE ESSENCE IN THREE PERSONS TAUGHT, IN SCRIPTURE, FROM THE FOUNDATION OF THE WORLD.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Beveridge’s summary.] This chapter has two parts. The first sets out the orthodox doctrine of the Holy Trinity. This runs from sec. 1-21, and may be divided into four heads: the first, on the meaning of Person, both the term and the thing meant by it, sec. 2-6; the second, proving the deity of the Son, sec. 7-13; the third, the deity of the Holy Spirit, sec. 14 and 15; and the fourth, explaining what is to be held concerning the Holy Trinity. The second part of the chapter refutes certain heresies that have arisen, particularly in our age, against this orthodox doctrine. This runs from sec. 21 to the end.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections (Beveridge’s summary).]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. Scripture, in teaching that the essence of God is immense and spiritual, refutes not only idolaters and the foolish wisdom of the world, but also the Manichees and Anthropomorphites. These last briefly refuted.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. In this one essence are three persons, yet in such a way that there is neither a threefold God nor a division of the simple essence of God. The meaning of the word Person in this discussion. Three hypostases in God, or the essence of God.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. The objection of those who, in this discussion, reject the use of the word Person. Answer 1. It is not a foreign term, but is used to explain sacred mysteries.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. Answer continued, 2. The orthodox forced to use the terms Trinity, Subsistence, and Person. Examples from the case of the Arians and Sabellians.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[5\. Answer continued, 3. The ancient Church, though differing somewhat in explaining these terms, agrees in substance. Proofs from Hilary, Jerome, and Augustine, in their use of the words Essence, Substance, Hypostasis. 4. Provided the orthodox meaning is kept, there should be no dispute over bare terms. But those who object to the terms usually favor the Arian and Sabellian heresy.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[6\. After the definition of the term comes a definition and explanation of the thing meant by it. The distinction of Persons.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[7\. Proofs of the eternal Deity of the Son. The Son the λόγος \[Word\] of the Eternal Father, and, therefore, the Son Eternal God. Objection. Reply.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[8\. The objection that the Λόγος began to be when God spoke in creating. Answer confirmed by Scripture and argument.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[9\. The Son called God and Jehovah. Other names of the Eternal Father applied to him in the Old Testament. He is, therefore, the Eternal God. Another objection refuted. The case of the Jews explained.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[10\. The angel who appeared to the fathers under the Law asserts that he is Jehovah. That angel was the Λόγος of the Eternal Father. The Son, being that Λόγος, is Eternal God. The impiety of Servetus refuted. Why the Son appeared in the form of an angel.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[11\. Passages from the New Testament in which the Son is acknowledged to be the Lord of Hosts, the Judge of the world, the God of glory, the Creator of the world, the Lord of angels, the King of the Church, the eternal Λόγος, God blessed forever, God manifested in the flesh, the equal of God, the true God and eternal life, the Lord and God of all believers. Therefore, the Eternal God.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[12\. Christ the Creator, Preserver, Redeemer, and Searcher of hearts. Therefore, the Eternal God.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[13\. Christ worked miracles by his own inherent power, and gave others the power to work them. Outside the Eternal God there is no salvation, no righteousness, no life. All these are in Christ. Christ, consequently, is the Eternal God. He in whom we believe and hope, to whom we pray, whom the Church acknowledges as the Savior of the faithful, whom to know is eternal life, in whom the godly glory, and through whom eternal blessings are imparted, is the Eternal God. Christ is all these, and, therefore, he is God.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[14\. The Divinity of the Spirit proved. I. He is the Creator and Preserver of the world. II. He sent the Prophets. III. He gives life to all things. IV. He is everywhere present. V. He renews the saints, and fits them for eternal life. VI. All the functions of Deity belong to him.]

