1\. #smallcaps[What] Scripture teaches about the immensity and the spirituality of the essence of God should serve not only to scatter the wild dreams of ordinary people, but also to refute the subtleties of a profane philosophy. One of the ancients thought he spoke shrewdly when he said that everything we see and everything we do not see is God.#footnote[Senec. #emph[Praef. lib.] 1 #emph[Quaest. Nat.] (Beveridge’s note.)] In this way he imagined that the Divinity was poured out into every separate part of the world. But although God, to keep us within the bounds of sobriety, speaks sparingly of his essence, yet by the two attributes I have mentioned he both does away with all crude imaginations and checks the audacity of the human mind. His immensity surely ought to deter us from measuring him by our own understanding, while his spiritual nature forbids us to indulge in any carnal or earthly speculation about him. For the same reason he often speaks of heaven as his dwelling place. It is true that, being incomprehensible, he fills the earth also; but knowing that our minds are slow and sink down to the earth, he raises us above the world, to shake off our sluggishness and inertia. And here we have a refutation of the error of the Manichees, who, by setting up two first principles, made the devil almost the equal of God. This, certainly, was both to destroy his unity and to restrict his immensity. Their attempt to twist certain passages of Scripture showed their shameful ignorance, just as the very nature of the error showed their monstrous madness. The Anthropomorphites also, who dreamed up a bodily God because Scripture often ascribes to him a mouth, ears, eyes, hands, and feet, are easily refuted. For who is so lacking in intelligence as not to understand that God, in speaking this way, lisps with us, as nurses do with little children? Such ways of speaking, therefore, do not so much express what kind of being God is, as accommodate the knowledge of him to our feebleness. To do this, he must of course stoop far below his true height.

2\. But there is also another special mark by which he identifies himself, to give us a closer knowledge of his nature. While he proclaims his unity, he sets himself before us to be considered distinctly in three persons. We must hold to these, or else only the bare and empty name of Deity will flutter about in our brain, without the true God. Moreover, so that no one may dream of a threefold God, or think that the simple essence is divided by the three Persons, we must look here for a brief and easy definition that will effectually guard us from error. But since some rail bitterly against the term Person as merely a human invention, let us first consider how far they have any ground for doing so.

When the Apostle calls the Son of God “the exact representation of His person” (Heb 1:3), he undoubtedly assigns to the Father some subsistence in which he differs from the Son. For to hold, with some interpreters, that the term means the same as essence (as if Christ represented the substance of the Father like the impression of a seal on wax) would be not only harsh but absurd. For since the essence of God is simple and undivided, the Son, who contains it in himself entire, in full perfection, without partition or diminution, would be called its exact representation (χαρακτὴρ) improperly, even ridiculously. But because the Father, though distinguished by his own property, has expressed himself wholly in the Son, he is said with perfect reason to have made his person (hypostasis) visible in him. And this fits well with what is added at once: that he is “the radiance of God’s glory.” From the Apostle’s words we rightly conclude that the Father has a subsistence (hypostasis) of his own, which shines out in the Son. From this, again, it is easy to infer that the Son has a subsistence (hypostasis) which distinguishes him from the Father. The same holds in the case of the Holy Spirit; for we will soon prove both that he is God, and yet that he must be held to be distinct from the Father. This, moreover, is not a distinction of essence, which it would be impious to multiply. If, then, the Apostle’s testimony is believed, it follows that there are three persons (hypostases) in God. Since the Latins have used the word #emph[Persona] to express the same thing as the Greek ὑπόστασις, it is excessively fastidious, and even stubborn, to wrangle about so clear a matter. The most literal translation would be #emph[subsistence]. Many have used #emph[substance] in the same sense. Nor, indeed, was the use of the term Person confined to the Latin Church. For the Greek Church likewise, perhaps to bear witness to their agreement, taught that there are three πρόσωπα \[persons\] in God. All these, however, whether Greeks or Latins, though they differ over the word, are in full agreement on the substance.

3\. Now, then, heretics may snarl, and the excessively fastidious may carp, at the word Person, refusing to admit it because it was devised by men. But since they cannot displace us from our position that three are named, each of whom is fully God, and yet that there are not several gods, it is most unfair to attack terms that do nothing more than explain what the Scriptures declare and confirm. “It would be better,” they say, “to keep not only our meanings but our words within the bounds of Scripture, and not to scatter foreign terms about, to become the seedbeds of future quarrels and dissensions. In this way men grow weary of fights about words; the truth is lost in wrangling, and love melts away in hateful strife.” If they call it a #emph[foreign] term because it cannot be found in Scripture syllable for syllable, they certainly impose an unjust law—a law that would condemn every interpretation of Scripture not put together from other words of Scripture. But if by #emph[foreign] they mean what is idly devised and then superstitiously defended, what tends more to strife than to edification, what is used either out of place or with no benefit, what offends pious ears by its harshness and leads them away from the simplicity of God’s Word, then I embrace their sobriety with all my heart. For I think we are bound to speak of God as reverently as we are bound to think of him. As our own thoughts about him are foolish, so our own language about him is absurd. Yet some measure must be kept. The unerring standard both of thinking and of speaking must be taken from the Scriptures: by it all the thoughts of our minds and the words of our mouths should be tested. But as for those parts of Scripture that are dark and intricate to our understanding, what forbids us to explain them in clearer terms—terms, however, kept in reverent and faithful service to the truth of Scripture, used sparingly and modestly, and not without need? There are many examples of this. And when it has been proved that the Church was driven by the strongest necessity to use the words Trinity and Person, will not anyone who still rails against new terms be rightly suspected of taking offense at the light of truth, as one whose only complaint is that the truth is made plain and clear?

4\. Such novelty (if it should be called novelty) is most needed when the truth must be maintained against slanderers who evade it by quibbling. We today know this all too well, having very great trouble in overcoming the enemies of pure and sound doctrine. These slippery snakes escape by their swift and twisting coils unless they are hotly pursued and, when caught, firmly held. So the early Christians, harassed by the disputes that heresies stirred up, were forced to declare what they believed in the most precise terms, so that no back doors might be left to ungodly men, for whom ambiguous words were a kind of hiding place. Arius confessed that Christ was God and the Son of God, because the passages of Scripture that say so were too clear to resist; and then, as if he had done his duty, he pretended to agree with the others. But all the while he did not stop claiming that Christ was created and had a beginning, like other creatures. To drag this man of wiles out of his hiding places, the ancient Church went a step further, and declared that Christ is the eternal Son of the Father, and consubstantial with the Father. The impiety came fully into the open when the Arians began to declare their hatred and utter loathing of the term ὁμοούσιος. If their first confession, that Christ was God, had been sincere and from the heart, they would not have denied that he was consubstantial with the Father. Who would dare to accuse those ancient writers of being quarrelsome and contentious for having debated so warmly, and disturbed the peace of the Church, over a single word? That little word distinguished between Christians of pure faith and the blasphemous Arians. Next Sabellius arose, who counted the names of Father, Son, and Holy Spirit as almost nothing, maintaining that they were not used to mark any distinction, but were different attributes of God, like many others of the same kind. When the matter came to debate, he acknowledged that he believed the Father was God, the Son God, the Spirit God; but then he had a way out ready: he had said nothing more than if he had called God powerful, and just, and wise. And so he sang another tune: that the Father was the Son, and the Holy Spirit the Father, without order or distinction. The good teachers of that time, who had piety at heart, proclaimed, to defeat this man’s dishonesty, that three properties must truly be acknowledged in the one God. And to arm themselves against his twisting craftiness with the simple, open truth, they affirmed that a Trinity of Persons truly subsisted in the one God, or (which is the same thing) in the unity of God.

5\. If, then, these names were not invented rashly, we must beware that in rejecting them we do not lay ourselves open to the charge of arrogance and rashness. I wish, indeed, that such names were buried, if only all would agree in the belief that the Father, Son, and Spirit are one God, and yet that the Son is not the Father, nor the Spirit the Son, but that they are distinguished by a certain property. I am not so rigidly precise as to fight fiercely over mere words. For I observe that the writers of the ancient Church, though they always spoke of these matters with great reverence, neither agreed with one another nor were always consistent with themselves. How strange the formulas used by councils, which Hilary excuses! How much license Augustine sometimes takes! How unlike the Greeks are to the Latins! But let one example of this variation be enough. When the Latins translated ὁμοούσιος, they used consubstantialis (consubstantial), indicating that there was one substance of the Father and the Son, and so using the word Substance for Essence. Hence Jerome, in his Letter to Damasus, says it is sacrilege to affirm that there are three substances in God. But in Hilary you will find it said more than a hundred times that there are three substances in God. Then how perplexed Jerome is over the word Hypostasis! He suspects some lurking poison when it is said that there are three Hypostases in God. And he does not hide his belief that the expression, even when used in a pious sense, is improper—if, indeed, he was sincere in saying this, and did not rather deliberately try, by an unfounded slander, to bring odium on the Eastern bishops, whom he hated. He certainly shows little candor in asserting that in all the pagan schools οὐσία \[essence\] means the same as Hypostasis—an assertion completely refuted by ordinary, everyday usage.

Augustine#footnote[#emph[De Trinit.] lib. 5 c. 8 and 9.] shows more courtesy and moderation. Although he says that Hypostasis in this sense is new to Latin ears, he is still so far from objecting to the Greeks’ ordinary use of the term that he even tolerates the Latins who had imitated the Greek way of speaking. What Socrates writes of the term, in the sixth book of the Tripartite History, comes to this: that unskilled men had wrongly applied it to this purpose. Hilary#footnote[#emph[De Trinitat.] lib. 2.] charges it against the heretics as a great crime that their wickedness had made it necessary to expose to the perils of human speech things that ought to have been kept reverently within the mind; and he does not hide his view that those who do so do what is unlawful, speak what cannot be spoken, and pry into what is forbidden. Shortly after, he excuses himself at great length for daring to bring in new terms. For after setting down the natural names, Father, Son, and Spirit, he adds that whatever is sought beyond these goes beyond the meaning of words, the perception of the senses, and the grasp of the intellect. And in another place#footnote[#emph[De Conciliis.]] he congratulates the bishops of France for not having framed any other confession, but having received, without change, the ancient and very simple confession received by all churches since the days of the apostles. Not unlike this is Augustine’s excuse, that the term had been wrung from him by necessity, because human language is so poor in so high a matter: not that the reality could be expressed by it, but so that he might not pass over in silence, without any attempt to show it, how the Father, Son, and Spirit are three.

The modesty of these holy men should warn us not to dip our pen in gall at once, and sternly denounce those who may be unwilling to swear to the terms we have devised—provided they do not do it out of pride, or insolence, or malicious craft, but are willing to weigh the necessity that compels us to speak so, and may thus gradually grow used to a useful form of expression. Let them also learn to take care that, when the Arians on the one side and the Sabellians on the other must be opposed, they do not, by taking offense that both are deprived of any handle for evasion, bring on themselves some suspicion of being disciples either of Arius or of Sabellius. Arius says that #emph[Christ is God], and then mutters that #emph[he was made and had a beginning]. He says that #emph[he is one with the Father], but secretly whispers in the ears of his followers, #emph[made one], like other believers, though with a special privilege. Say #emph[he is consubstantial], and you at once pull the mask off this chameleon, though you add nothing to Scripture. Sabellius says that #emph[the Father, Son, and Spirit indicate no distinction in God]. Say #emph[they are three], and he will shout that you are making three Gods. Say that #emph[there is a Trinity of Persons in one divine essence], and you will only have said in one word what the Scriptures say, and silenced his empty chatter. If any are so superstitiously scrupulous that they cannot bear these terms, still, do what they will, they will not be able to deny that when we hear of #emph[one], a unity of substance is to be understood, and when we hear of #emph[three] in one essence, the persons in this Trinity are meant. When this is confessed without equivocation, we do not dwell on words. But I learned long ago, and indeed more than once, that those who stubbornly quarrel about words are tainted with some hidden poison; and therefore that it is better to provoke them on purpose than to court their favor by speaking obscurely.

6\. But to say nothing more of words, let us now turn to the thing itself. By #emph[person], then, I mean a subsistence in the divine essence—a subsistence which, while related to the other two, is distinguished from them by an incommunicable property. By #emph[subsistence] we mean something other than #emph[essence]. For if the Word were simply God, and had no property of his own, John could not rightly have said that he had always been with God (John 1:1). When he adds immediately after that the Word was God, he calls us back to the one essence. But because he could not be with God without dwelling in the Father, from this arises that subsistence which, though joined to the essence by an indissoluble bond and incapable of being separated from it, yet has a special mark by which it is distinguished from it. Now I say that each of the three subsistences, while related to the others, is distinguished by its own property. Relation is expressly stated here because, when God is mentioned simply and without qualification, the name belongs no less to the Son and the Spirit than to the Father. But whenever the Father is compared with the Son, each one’s own property distinguishes the one from the other. Third, whatever is proper to each I affirm to be incommunicable, because nothing that is attributed to the Father as a mark of distinction can apply or be transferred to the Son. Nor do I object to adopting Tertullian’s definition, provided it is rightly understood: “that there is in God a certain arrangement or economy, which makes no change in the unity of essence.”#footnote[Tertull. #emph[Lib. contra Praxeam.]]

7\. Before going further, we must prove the Divinity of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. After that, we will see how they differ from each other. When the Word of God is set before us in the Scriptures, it would certainly be most absurd to imagine that it is only a fleeting and passing voice, sent out into the air and going forth outside God himself, as were the oracles given to the patriarchs and all the prophecies. It means, rather, the wisdom always dwelling with God, by which all oracles and prophecies were inspired. For, as Peter testifies (1 Pet 1:11), the ancient prophets spoke by the Spirit of Christ just as the apostles did, and all who after them were ministers of the heavenly doctrine. But since Christ had not yet been revealed, we must understand that the Word was begotten of the Father before all ages. And if that Spirit, whose instruments the prophets were, belonged to the Word, we cannot escape the conclusion that the Word was truly God. Moses shows this clearly enough in his account of the creation, where he places the Word as the intermediary. For why does he expressly tell us that God, in creating each of his works, said, Let there be this—let there be that, unless it was so that the unsearchable glory of God might shine forth in his image? I know that chatterers could easily evade this by saying that #emph[Word] is used for #emph[order] or #emph[command]\; but the apostles are better interpreters when they tell us that the worlds were created by the Son, and that he sustains all things by his mighty word (Heb 1:2). For here we see that #emph[word] is used for the bidding or command of the Son, who is himself the eternal and essential Word of the Father. And no man of sound mind can doubt Solomon’s meaning when he presents Wisdom as begotten by God before all ages, and presiding over the creation of the world and all the other works of God (Prov 8:22). For it would be foolish and trifling to imagine some temporary command at a time when God was pleased to carry out his fixed and eternal counsel, and something still more hidden. Our Savior’s words point to this: “To this very day My Father is at His work, and I too am working” (John 5:17). In saying that from the foundation of the world he has always worked with the Father, he explains more clearly what Moses only touched on. The meaning, then, is that God spoke in such a way that the Word had his own part in the work, and so the work was common to both. But the clearest explanation is given by John (John 1:3), when he states that the Word—who was from the beginning God and with God—was, together with God the Father, the maker of all things. For he both ascribes to the Word a substantial and permanent essence, assigning to him something of his own, and shows plainly how God spoke the world into being. Therefore, as all revelations from heaven are rightly given the title of the Word of God, so the highest place must be given to that substantial Word, the source of all inspiration, who, being subject to no change, remains forever one and the same with God, and is God.

8\. Here certain dogs raise an outcry: while they dare not openly deny his divinity, they secretly rob him of his eternity. For they contend that the Word only began to be when God opened his sacred mouth in the creation of the world. So, with excessive rashness, they imagine some change in the essence of God. For just as the names of God that refer to his outward works began to be ascribed to him only once the work existed (as when he is called the Creator of heaven and earth), so piety does not recognize or admit any name that would indicate that a change had taken place in God himself. For if anything new had come to him, the saying of James would fail, that “every good and perfect gift is from above, coming down from the Father of the heavenly lights, with whom there is no change or shifting shadow” (Jas 1:17). Nothing, therefore, is more intolerable than to imagine a beginning for that Word who was always God, and afterwards was the Creator of the world. But they think they argue cleverly in maintaining that when Moses says that God then spoke for the first time, he must be taken to imply that until then there was no Word in him. Nothing could be more trifling. Surely it does not follow that because a thing begins to be revealed at a certain time, it never existed before. I draw a very different conclusion. Since at the very moment God said, “Let there be light,” the power of the Word was at once put forth, the Word must have existed long before. If anyone asks how long before, he will find it was without beginning. He himself sets no fixed span of time when he says, “And now, Father, glorify Me in Your presence with the glory I had with You before the world existed” (John 17:5). Nor did John leave this out: for before he comes down to the creation of the world, he says that “in the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God.” We therefore conclude again that the Word was eternally begotten by God, and dwelt with him from everlasting. In this way his true essence, his eternity, and his divinity are established.

9\. Although I am not yet dealing with the office of the Mediator, but am putting it off until we come to the subject of redemption, yet because it should be clear and beyond dispute for all that Christ is that Word made flesh, this seems the most fitting place to bring in the passages that assert the Divinity of Christ. When it is said in the forty-fifth Psalm, “Your throne, O God, endures forever and ever,” the Jews quibble that the name Elohim is applied to angels and to the highest powers. But nowhere in Scripture is there a passage that sets up an eternal throne for a created being. For he is not simply called God, but also the eternal Ruler. Besides, the title is not given to any man without some qualification, as when it is said that Moses would be a God to Pharaoh (Exod 7:1). Some read it as a genitive, but this is too insipid. I admit that anything of rare excellence is often called divine; but it is clear from the context that this meaning would be harsh and forced here, and does not fit at all. But if their stubbornness still refuses to yield, surely there is no obscurity in Isaiah, where Christ is presented both as God and as endowed with supreme power, which belongs to God alone: “His name will be called the Mighty God, the Everlasting Father, the Prince of Peace” (Isa 9:6). Here too the Jews object, and turn the reading around thus: This is the name by which the Mighty God, the Everlasting Father, will call him; so that all they leave to the Son is “Prince of Peace.” But why would so many titles be heaped on God the Father here, when the prophet’s purpose is to present the Messiah with certain distinguishing marks that may lead us to put our faith in him? There can be no doubt, therefore, that he who a little before was called Immanuel is here called the Mighty God. Moreover, nothing could be clearer than the words of Jeremiah concerning the Branch of David: “This is His name by which He will be called: The #smallcaps[Lord] \[Jehovah\] Our Righteousness” (Jer 23:6). For since the Jews themselves teach that God’s other names are only epithets, whereas this one, which they call the ineffable name, is a substantive and expresses his essence, we infer that the Son is the only and eternal God, who elsewhere declares, “I will not give My glory to another” (Isa 42:8). They try to escape this by pointing out that Moses gave this name to the altar he built, and Ezekiel to the new Jerusalem. But who does not see that the altar was built as a memorial to show that God was the one who exalted Moses, and that the name of God was given to Jerusalem only to bear witness to the divine presence? For the prophet speaks thus: “From that day on the name of the city will be: The #smallcaps[Lord] \[Jehovah\] Is There” (Ezek 48:35). In the same way, “Moses built an altar and named it Jehovah-nissi” (Jehovah my exaltation) (Exod 17:15). But it seems the point is disputed even more sharply over another passage in Jeremiah, where the same title is applied to Jerusalem in these words: “In those days Judah will be saved, and Jerusalem will dwell securely, and this is the name by which it will be called: The #smallcaps[Lord] \[Jehovah\] Our Righteousness” (Jer 33:16). But this passage is so far from opposing the truth we defend that it rather supports it. Having earlier declared that Christ is the true Jehovah, from whom righteousness flows, the prophet now declares that the Church would be made so aware of this that it could glory in taking his very name. So in the former passage the fountain and cause of righteousness is set down; in the latter, the effect is described.

10\. But if this does not satisfy the Jews, I do not see what quibbles will let them evade the many passages in which Jehovah is said to have appeared in the form of an Angel (Judg 6; 7; 13:16–23, etc.). This Angel claims for himself the name of the Eternal God. If someone objects that this is said with respect to the office he bears, the difficulty is by no means solved. No servant would rob God of his honor by allowing sacrifice to be offered to himself. But the Angel refuses to eat bread, and orders the sacrifice to be offered to Jehovah. Then he proves by the deed itself that he was truly Jehovah. Accordingly, Manoah and his wife infer from the sign that they had seen not only an angel, but God. Hence Manoah’s cry, “We will die, for we have seen God.” When his wife replies, “If Jehovah had wished to kill us, he would not have accepted the sacrifice from our hand,” she acknowledges that he who was earlier called an angel was certainly God. Add to this that the angel’s own reply removes all doubt: “Why do you ask my name, which is wonderful?” So the impiety of Servetus was all the more detestable when he maintained that God was never revealed to Abraham and the patriarchs, but that an angel was worshiped in his place. The orthodox teachers of the Church have rightly and wisely explained that the Word of God was that supreme angel, who even then began, as it were by anticipation, to perform the office of Mediator. For though he was not yet clothed with flesh, he came down as an intermediary, so that he might come to the faithful more familiarly. This closer fellowship gained him the name of the Angel; yet he kept the character that rightly belonged to him, that of the God of ineffable glory. Hosea says the same thing when, after mentioning Jacob’s wrestling with the angel, he says, “the #smallcaps[Lord] is the God of Hosts—the #smallcaps[Lord] is His name of renown” (Hos 12:5). Servetus again insinuates that God played the part of an angel—as if the prophet did not confirm what Moses had said: “Why do you ask my name?” (Gen 32:29, 30). And the holy patriarch’s confession shows clearly enough that he was not a created angel, but one in whom the fullness of the Godhead dwelt, when he says, “I have seen God face to face.” Hence also Paul’s statement that Christ led the people in the wilderness (1 Cor 10:4).#footnote[See also Calvin on Acts 7:30, and below, ch. 14, s. 9. (Beveridge’s note.)] Although the time of his humbling had not yet come, the eternal Word set forth a figure of the office he was to fulfill. Again, if the second chapter of Zechariah (v. 3, etc.) is fairly considered, it will be seen that the angel who sends the other angel is declared immediately afterwards to be the Lord of hosts, and that supreme power is ascribed to him. I pass over countless passages in which our faith rests secure, though they may not carry much weight with the Jews. For when it is said in Isaiah, “Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him, and He will save us. This is the #smallcaps[Lord] \[Jehovah\] for whom we have waited. Let us rejoice and be glad in His salvation” (Isa 25:9), anyone with eyes can see that the God meant is he who rises up again to deliver his people. And the emphatic pointing, given twice, rules out any reference to anyone other than Christ. Still clearer and stronger is the passage of Malachi, in which a promise is made that the Lord who was then sought would come to his own temple (Mal 3:1). The temple was certainly dedicated to Almighty God alone, and yet the prophet claims it for Christ. From this it follows that he is the God whom the Jews always worshiped.

11\. The New Testament teems with countless passages, so our aim must be to select a few rather than to pile up all of them. And although the Apostles spoke of him as Mediator after he had appeared in the flesh, every passage I bring forward will be enough to prove his eternal Godhead. The first thing that deserves special notice is that predictions concerning the eternal God are applied to Christ, as either already fulfilled in him or to be fulfilled at some future time. Isaiah prophesies that “the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts” will be “a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense” (Isa 8:14). Paul asserts that this prophecy was fulfilled in Christ (Rom 9:33), and therefore declares that Christ is that Lord of Hosts. In the same way, he says in another passage (Rom 14:10), “We will all stand before the judgment seat of Christ. For it is written: ‘As surely as I live, says the Lord, every knee will bow before Me; every tongue will confess to God.’” Since in Isaiah God foretells this of himself (Isa 45:23), and Christ shows it fulfilled in himself, it follows that he is that very God whose glory cannot be given to another. It is clear also that the passage from the Psalms (Ps 68:19) which he quotes in the Epistle to the Ephesians applies only to God: “When He ascended on high, He led captives away” (Eph 4:8). Understanding that such an ascension was foreshadowed when the Lord displayed his power and won a glorious victory over heathen nations, he indicates that what was then foreshadowed was more fully revealed in Christ. So John testifies that it was the glory of the Son that was revealed to Isaiah in a vision (John 12:41; Isa 6:1), though Isaiah himself expressly says that what he saw was the Majesty of God. Again, there can be no doubt that the qualities which the Epistle to the Hebrews applies to the Son are the brightest attributes of God: “In the beginning, O Lord, You laid the foundations of the earth,” etc., and, “Let all God’s angels worship Him” (Heb 1:10, 6). Yet he does not misuse these passages in applying them to Christ, since Christ alone did the things these passages celebrate. It was he who arose and had mercy on Zion—he who claimed for himself dominion over all nations and islands. And why should John have hesitated to ascribe the Majesty of God to Christ (John 1:14), after saying in his preface that the Word was God? Why should Paul have been afraid to set Christ on the judgment seat of God (2 Cor 5:10), when he had already proclaimed his divinity so openly by saying that he was God over all, blessed forever (Rom 9:5)? And to show how consistent he is on this point, he says elsewhere that “God appeared in the flesh” (1 Tim 3:16). If he is God, blessed forever, then he is the one to whom alone, as Paul affirms in another place, all glory and honor are due (1 Tim 1:17). Paul does not hide this, but openly declares that, “existing in the form of God,” he “did not consider it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied Himself” (Phil 2:6). And so that the wicked could not clamor that he was some kind of made God, John goes further and affirms, “He is the true God and eternal life” (1 John 5:20). And yet it ought to be enough for us that he is called God, especially by a witness who expressly testifies that we have no more gods than one. Paul says, “For even if there are so-called gods, whether in heaven or on earth (as there are many so-called gods and lords), yet for us there is but one God” (1 Cor 8:5, 6). When we hear from the same lips that God appeared in the flesh, and that God purchased the Church with his own blood (Acts 20:28), why do we dream of some second God, of whom he makes not the slightest mention? And there is no room to doubt that all the godly held the same view. Thomas, by calling him his Lord and God, certainly professes that he was the only God he had ever worshiped (John 20:28).

12\. The divinity of Christ, if judged by the works ascribed to him in Scripture, becomes still more evident. When he said of himself, “To this very day My Father is at His work, and I too am working,” the Jews, though utterly dull about his other sayings, saw that he was laying claim to divine power. And therefore, as John relates (John 5:17), they tried all the harder to kill him, because he not only broke the Sabbath, but also said that God was his Father, making himself equal with God. What, then, will our stupidity be if we do not see from this same passage that his divinity is plainly asserted? To govern the world by his power and providence, and to rule all things by a power inherent in himself (this an Apostle ascribes to him, Heb 1:3), surely belongs to none but the Creator. Nor does he share with the Father only the government of the world, but also each of the other functions, which cannot be imparted to created beings. The Lord proclaims by his prophet, “I, yes I, am He who blots out your transgressions for My own sake” (Isa 43:25). When, on the strength of this declaration, the Jews thought that God was wronged when Christ forgave sins, he not only asserted in plain words that this power belonged to him, but also proved it by a miracle (Matt 9:6). So we see that he had in himself not the ministry of forgiving sins, but the inherent power which the Lord declares he will not give to another. What! Does it not belong to God alone to search and penetrate the secret thoughts of the heart? But Christ also had this power (Matt 9:4), and therefore we infer that Christ is God.

13\. How clearly and plainly this appears in his miracles! I admit that the prophets and apostles performed similar and equal miracles; but there is this very essential difference: they dispensed the gifts of God as his ministers, whereas he put forth his own inherent might. Sometimes, indeed, he prayed, to give glory to the Father; but we see that for the most part it is his own power that is displayed. And how could he not be the true author of miracles who, on his own authority, commissions others to perform them? For the Evangelist relates (Matt 10:8; Mark 3:15; 6:7) that he gave the apostles power to cast out devils, heal the lepers, raise the dead, and so on. And by the way they carried out this ministry, they showed plainly that their whole power came from Christ. “In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth,” says Peter (Acts 3:6), “get up and walk!” It is not surprising, then, that Christ appealed to his miracles to overcome the unbelief of the Jews, since these were done by his own power, and so bore the fullest testimony to his divinity (John 5:36; 14:11; 10:37).

Again, if apart from God there is no salvation, no righteousness, no life, then Christ, who has all these in himself, is certainly God. Let no one object that life or salvation is transfused into him by God. For it is not said that he received salvation, but that he himself is salvation. And if there is no one good but God (Matt 19:17), how could a mere man be, I do not say good and just, but goodness and justice itself? Then what shall we say to the testimony of the Evangelist, that from the very beginning of the creation “in Him was life, and that life was the light of men” (John 1:4)? Trusting in such proofs, we can boldly put our hope and faith in him, though we know it is blasphemous impiety to put our trust in any created thing.#footnote[The French adds, “Et ne faisons point cela témérairement, mais selon sa parole.”—And let us not do this rashly, but in accordance with his Word. (Beveridge’s note.)] “You#super[+] believe in God,”#footnote[Calvin translates it as a question, “Do you#super[+] believe in God?” (Beveridge’s note.)] he says, “believe in Me as well” (John 14:1). And so Paul (Rom 10:11, and 15:12) interprets two passages of Isaiah: “Anyone who believes in Him will never be put to shame” (Isa 28:16); and, “There will be a root of Jesse, and he who will rise to rule over the Gentiles; in him the Gentiles will trust” (Isa 11:10). But why bring forward more passages of Scripture on this point, when we so often meet with the saying, “He who believes in Me has eternal life” (John 6:47)?

Again, the prayer of faith is addressed to him—prayer, which belongs especially to the divine majesty, if anything does. For the prophet Joel says, “And everyone who calls on the name of the #smallcaps[Lord] (Jehovah) will be saved” (Joel 2:32). And another says, “The name of the #smallcaps[Lord] (Jehovah) is a strong tower; the righteous run to it and are safe” (Prov 18:10). But the name of Christ is called on for salvation, and therefore it follows that he is Jehovah. Moreover, we have an example of such calling on him in Stephen, when he said, “Lord Jesus, receive my spirit” (Acts 7:59); and then in the whole Church, when Ananias says in the same book, “Lord, many people have told me about this man and all the harm he has done to Your saints in Jerusalem. And now he is here with authority from the chief priests to arrest all who call on Your name” (Acts 9:13, 14). And to make it more clearly understood that the whole fullness of the Godhead dwelt bodily in Christ, the Apostle declares that the only doctrine he professed among the Corinthians, the only doctrine he taught, was the knowledge of Christ (1 Cor 2:2). Consider what kind of thing it is, and how great, that the name of the Son alone is preached to us, though God commands us to glory only in the knowledge of himself (Jer 9:24). Who will dare to maintain that he, the knowledge of whom is our only ground of glorying, is a mere creature? To this we may add that the greetings at the head of Paul’s Epistles pray for the same blessings from the Son as from the Father. By this we are taught not only that the blessings our heavenly Father bestows come to us through the Son’s intercession, but that, by a partnership in power, the Son himself is their author. This practical knowledge is without doubt surer and more solid than any idle speculation. For the pious soul beholds God most present, and may almost be said to touch him, when it feels that it is made alive, enlightened, saved, justified, and sanctified by him.

14\. In asserting the divinity of the Spirit, the proof must be drawn from the same sources. And it is by no means an obscure testimony that Moses gives in the history of the creation, when he says (Gen 1:2) that the Spirit of God was spread over the abyss, or shapeless matter; for it shows not only that the beauty the world displays is maintained by the invigorating power of the Spirit, but that even before this beauty existed the Spirit was at work nurturing the confused mass.#footnote[The French adds, “à ce qu’elle ne fust point aneantie incontinent;”—so as to keep it from being annihilated at once. (Beveridge’s note.)] Again, no quibbles can explain away the force of what Isaiah says, “And now the Lord #smallcaps[God] and His Spirit have sent me” (Isa 48:16), so ascribing to the Holy Spirit a share in the sovereign power of sending the prophets.#footnote[Calvin on Acts 20:28. (Beveridge’s note.)] In this his divine majesty shines out clearly.

But, as I have said, the best proof for us is our own familiar experience. For nothing can be more foreign to a created being than the office which the Scriptures ascribe to him, and which the pious actually feel him performing: he is spread through all space, sustaining, invigorating, and giving life to all things, both in heaven and on earth. The very fact that he is not bounded by any limits raises him above the rank of created beings, while his pouring vigor into all things, breathing being, life, and motion into them, is plainly divine. Again, if regeneration to incorruptible life is higher and far more excellent than any present giving of life, what must we think of him by whose power it is produced? Now, many passages of Scripture show that he is the author of regeneration, not by a borrowed but by his own inherent power; and not only so, but that he is also the author of our future immortality. In short, all the distinctive attributes of the Godhead are ascribed to him in the same way as to the Son. He searches the deep things of God (1 Cor 2:10), and has no counselor among created beings; he gives wisdom and the faculty of speech, though God declares to Moses (Exod 4:11) that this belongs to him alone. In the same way, through him we become partakers of the divine nature, so that we feel his life-giving power, in a manner, within us. Our justification is his work; from him come power, sanctification, truth, grace, and whatever good can be conceived, since there is one Spirit from whom every kind of gift flows. Paul’s statement deserves particular attention: that though there are different gifts, and a manifold and varied distribution, there is yet the same Spirit (1 Cor 12:11, etc.), he being not only the beginning or origin, but also the author;#footnote[The French adds, “Sainct Paul n’eust jamais ainsi parlé, s’il n’eust cognu la vraie Divinité du Sainct Esprit”—St Paul would never have spoken this way if he had not known the divinity of the Holy Spirit. (Beveridge’s note.)] as is expressed even more clearly just after, in these words: “who apportions them to each one as He wills.” For were he not something subsisting in God, will and free choice would never be ascribed to him. Most clearly, therefore, does Paul ascribe divine power to the Spirit, and show that he dwells hypostatically in God.

15\. Nor does Scripture, in speaking of him, withhold the name of God. Paul infers that we are the temple of God from the fact that “the Spirit of God dwells in us” (1 Cor 3:16; 6:19; and 2 Cor 6:16). Now this ought not to be lightly passed over: all the promises God makes of choosing us for himself as a temple are fulfilled only by his Spirit dwelling in us. Surely, as Augustine admirably puts it,#footnote[#emph[Ad Maximinum], Ep. 66.] “if we were ordered to make a temple of wood and stone for the Spirit, since such worship is due to God alone, it would be a clear proof of the Spirit’s divinity; how much clearer a proof, then, that we are not to make a temple for him, but to be that temple ourselves.” And the Apostle says at one time that we are the temple of God, and at another, in the same sense, that we are the temple of the Holy Spirit. Peter, when he rebuked Ananias for having lied to the Holy Spirit, said that he had not lied to men, but to God (Acts 5:3, 4). And when Isaiah had brought in the Lord of Hosts as speaking, Paul says that it was the Holy Spirit who spoke (Isa 6:9; Acts 28:25, 26). Indeed, words which the prophets constantly say were spoken by the Lord of Hosts are ascribed by Christ and his apostles to the Holy Spirit. Hence it follows that the Spirit is the true Jehovah, who is the principal author of the prophecies. Again, where God complains that he was provoked to anger by the stubbornness of the people, Isaiah says instead that his Holy Spirit was grieved (Isa 63:10). Lastly, while blasphemy against the Spirit is not forgiven, either in the present life or in the life to come (Matt 12:31; Mark 3:29; Luke 12:10), whereas he who has blasphemed against the Son may obtain pardon, that majesty must certainly be divine which it is an unpardonable crime to offend or impair. I deliberately pass over many passages which the ancient Fathers brought forward. They thought it plausible to quote from David, “By the word of the #smallcaps[Lord] the heavens were made, and all the stars by the breath (Spirit) of His mouth” (Ps 33:6), to prove that the world was no less the work of the Holy Spirit than of the Son. But since it is usual in the Psalms to say the same thing twice, and in Isaiah the #emph[spirit] (breath) of the mouth means the same as #emph[word] (Isa 11:4), that proof was weak; and so my wish has been to touch briefly on those proofs on which pious minds may firmly rest.

16\. But as God has revealed himself more clearly by the coming of Christ, so he has made himself more familiarly known in three persons. Out of many proofs, this one will be enough. Paul links together these three, God, Faith, and Baptism (Eph 4:5), and reasons from the one to the other—namely, because there is one faith he infers that there is one God; and because there is one baptism he infers that there is one faith. Therefore, if by baptism we are initiated into the faith and worship of one God, we must necessarily believe that the one into whose name we are baptized is the true God. And there can be no doubt that our Savior wished to testify, by a solemn declaration, that the perfect light of faith is now displayed, when he said, “Go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit” (Matt 28:19), for this is the same as being baptized into the name of the one God, who has been fully revealed in the Father, the Son, and the Spirit. From this it is plain that the three persons, in whom the one God is known, subsist in the divine essence. And since faith certainly ought not to look here and there, or run up and down after various objects, but to look to God alone, refer itself to him, and cling to him, it is obvious that if there were various kinds of faith, there would also have to be various gods. Then, as baptism is the sacrament of faith, its unity assures us of the unity of God. This also proves that we may be baptized only into one God, because we make a profession of faith in him into whose name we are baptized. What, then, does our Savior mean when he commands baptism to be administered in the name of the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit, if not that we are to believe with one faith in the name of the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit?#footnote[The French entirely omits the three previous sentences, beginning, “Then, as,” etc. (Beveridge’s note.)] And what is this but to declare that the Father, Son, and Spirit are one God? Therefore, since it must be held certain that there is one God, not more than one, we conclude that the Word and Spirit are no other than the very essence of God. Nothing could be more stupid than the trifling of the Arians, who, while acknowledging the divinity of the Son, denied his divine essence. Equally wild were the ravings of the Macedonians, who insisted that by the Spirit only the gifts of grace poured out on men were meant. For as wisdom, understanding, prudence, fortitude, and the fear of the Lord proceed from the Spirit, so he is the one Spirit of wisdom, prudence, fortitude, and piety. He is not divided according to the distribution of his gifts, but, as the Apostle assures us (1 Cor 12:11), however they are divided, he remains one and the same.

17\. On the other hand, the Scriptures show that there is some distinction between the Father and the Word, and between the Word and the Spirit; but the very greatness of the mystery reminds us how much reverence and sobriety we must bring to discussing it. To me nothing seems more admirable than the words of Gregory Nazianzen: “Οὐ φθάνω τὸ ἓν νοῆσαι, καὶ τοῖς τρισὶ περιλάμπομαι· οὐ φθάνω τὰ τρία διελεῖν καὶ εἰς τὸ ἓν ἀναφέρομαι” (Greg. Nazianz. in Serm. de Sacro Baptis.). “I cannot think of the one without being instantly lit up by the three: I cannot distinguish the three without being at once carried up to the one.”#footnote[Bernard, De Consider. lib. 5 “Cum dico unum, non me trinitatis turbat numerus, qui essentiam non multiplicat, non variat, nec partitur. Rursum, quum, dico tria, non me arguit intuitus unitatis, quia illa quæcunque tria, seu illos tres, nec in confusionem cogit, nec in singularitatem redigit.” \[When I say one, the number of the Trinity does not trouble me, for it does not multiply the essence, or vary it, or divide it. Again, when I say three, the sight of the unity does not rebuke me, for it neither forces those three things, whatever they are, or those three persons, into confusion, nor reduces them to singleness.\]—See also Bernard, Serm. 71 in Cantica. (Beveridge’s note.)] Therefore let us beware of imagining such a Trinity of persons as will pull our thoughts apart, instead of bringing them at once back to the unity. The words Father, Son, and Holy Spirit certainly indicate a real distinction, so that we may not suppose they are mere titles by which God is variously designated from his works. Still, they indicate distinction only, not division. The passages we have already quoted show that the Son has a property distinct from the Father, because the Word could not have been with God unless he were distinct from the Father; nor could he otherwise have had his glory with the Father. In the same way, Christ distinguishes the Father from himself when he says that there is another who bears witness of him (John 5:32; 8:16). To the same effect it is said elsewhere that the Father made all things by the Word. This could not be, if the Father were not in some respect distinct from the Word. Besides, it was not the Father who came down to the earth, but he who came forth from the Father; nor was it the Father who died and rose again, but he whom the Father had sent. This distinction did not take its beginning at the incarnation: for it is clear that the only begotten Son existed before that in the bosom of the Father (John 1:18). For who will dare to affirm that the Son entered his Father’s bosom for the first time when he came down from heaven to take on human nature? He was therefore in the bosom of the Father before, and had his glory with the Father. Christ indicates the distinction between the Holy Spirit and the Father when he says that the Spirit proceeds from the Father, and between the Holy Spirit and himself whenever he speaks of him as another, as he does when he declares that he will send another Comforter; and in many other passages besides (John 15:26; 14:16).

18\. I am not sure whether it is wise to borrow analogies from human affairs to express the nature of this distinction. The ancient Fathers sometimes do so, but at the same time they admit that what they bring forward as an analogy is very widely different. And so I greatly dread anything like presumption here, lest some rash saying should give the malicious an occasion for slander, or the unlearned an occasion for delusion. It would not be right, however, to say nothing of a distinction which we see the Scriptures have pointed out. This distinction is that to the Father is attributed the beginning of action, the fountain and source of all things; to the Son, wisdom, counsel, and the ordering of action; while the energy and efficacy of action is assigned to the Spirit. Moreover, though the eternity of the Father is also the eternity of the Son and Spirit, since God never could be without his own wisdom and energy, and though in eternity there can be no room for first or last, still the distinction of order is not meaningless or superfluous: the Father is considered first, next the Son from him, and then the Spirit from both. For the mind of every man naturally inclines to consider first God, secondly the wisdom coming forth from him, and lastly the energy by which he carries out the purposes of his counsel. For this reason the Son is said to be of the Father only; the Spirit of both the Father and the Son. This is done in many passages, but in none more clearly than in the eighth chapter of Romans (Rom 8:9), where the same Spirit is called without distinction the Spirit of Christ and the Spirit of him who raised up Christ from the dead. And rightly so. For Peter also testifies (1 Pet 1:11) that it was the Spirit of Christ who inspired the prophets, though the Scriptures so often say that it was the Spirit of God the Father.

19\. Moreover, this distinction is so far from interfering with the most perfect unity of God that by it the Son can be proved to be one God with the Father, because he has one and the same Spirit with him; and the Spirit can be proved to be no different from the Father and the Son, because he is the Spirit of the Father and the Son. In each hypostasis the whole nature is understood, with this addition, that each has his own particular property. The whole Father is in the Son, and the whole Son in the Father, as the Son himself also declares (John 14:10), “I am in the Father and the Father is in Me”; nor do the church’s writers admit that the one is separated from the other by any difference of essence. “By those names which denote distinction,” says Augustine, “what is meant is the relation they bear to each other, not the very substance by which they are one.” This is how we should reconcile the views of the Fathers, which might sometimes seem to be at odds with each other. At one time they teach that the Father is the beginning of the Son; at another they assert that the Son has both divinity and essence from himself, and is therefore one beginning with the Father. The cause of this difference is well and clearly explained by Augustine when he says,#footnote[August. Homil. De Temp. 38, De Trinitate. See also Ad Pascentium Epist. 174 Cyrill. De Trinit. lib. 7; Idem, lib. 3 Dialog.; Aug. in Ps 109; et Tract. in Joann. 39; Idem, in Ps 68.] “Christ, as to himself, is called God; as to the Father, he is called Son.” And again, “The Father, as to himself, is called God; as to the Son, he is called Father. He who, as to the Son, is called Father, is not Son; he who, as to the Father, is called Son, is not Father; and what the Father is called as to himself, and the Son as to himself, is the same God.” Therefore, when we speak of the Son simply, without reference to the Father, we truly and properly affirm that he is of himself, and so call him the only beginning; but when we denote the relation which he bears to the Father, we rightly make the Father the beginning of the Son. Augustine’s fifth book on the Trinity is wholly given to explaining this subject. But it is far safer to rest content with the relation as he teaches it than to get lost in vain speculation by subtle prying into a sublime mystery.

20\. Let those, then, who love sobriety and are content with the measure of faith, receive in brief what is useful to know. It is this: when we profess to believe in one God, the name God is understood to mean the one simple essence, comprehending three persons or hypostases; and so, whenever the name of God is used without qualification, the Son and Spirit are meant no less than the Father. But when the Son is joined with the Father, relation comes into view, and so we distinguish between the Persons. But as the properties in the Persons carry an order with them, the principle and origin being in the Father, whenever the Father and the Son, or the Father and the Spirit, are mentioned together, the name of God is specially given to the Father. In this way the unity of essence is retained, and account is taken of the order, which, however, takes nothing away from the divinity of the Son and Spirit. And surely, since we have already seen that the apostles declare the Son of God to be He whom Moses and the prophets declared to be Jehovah, we must always come back to a unity of essence. We therefore hold it detestable blasphemy to call the Son a different God from the Father, because the simple name God admits no relation, nor can God, considered in himself, be said to be this or that. Then, that the name Jehovah, taken without qualification, may be applied to Christ is clear from the words of Paul: “Three times I pleaded with the Lord.” After giving the answer, “My grace is sufficient for you,” he adds, “so that the power of Christ may rest on me” (2 Cor 12:8, 9). For it is certain that the name of Lord (Κυρίου) is put there for Jehovah, and so to restrict it to the person of the Mediator would be childish and trivial, since the words are used absolutely, and not to compare the Father with the Son. And we know that, following the accepted usage of the Greeks, the apostles regularly put the word Κύριος in place of Jehovah. Not to go far for an example, Paul pleaded with the Lord in the same sense in which Peter quotes the passage of Joel, “Everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved” (Acts 2:21; Joel 2:28). Where this name is specially applied to the Son, there is a different reason for it, as will be seen in its own place; for the present it is enough to remember that Paul, after praying to God absolutely, immediately adds the name of Christ. Thus, too, God in his entirety is called Spirit by Christ himself. For there is nothing to hinder the essence of God, in which Father, Son, and Spirit are comprehended, from being wholly spiritual. This is plain from Scripture. For as God is there called Spirit, so the Holy Spirit also, insofar as he is a hypostasis of the whole essence, is said to be both of God and from God.

21\. But Satan, in order to tear up our faith by the roots, has always stirred up fierce disputes, partly concerning the divine essence of the Son and Spirit, and partly concerning the distinction of persons; in almost every age he has raised up ungodly spirits to harass the orthodox teachers on this point, and in the present day he is trying to kindle a new flame out of the old embers. For these reasons it will be proper here to deal with some of these perverse dreams. So far our chief aim has been to stretch out a guiding hand to those who are willing to learn, not to do battle with the stubborn and contentious; but now the truth which was calmly shown must be defended against the slanders of the ungodly. Still, our chief concern will be to give a sure footing to those whose ears are open to the word of God. Here, if anywhere, in considering the hidden mysteries of Scripture, we should reflect soberly and with great moderation, carefully guarding against letting either our mind or our tongue go a step beyond the bounds of God’s word. For how can the human mind, which has not yet been able to determine what the body of the sun consists of, though it sees it every day, bring down the boundless essence of God to its own little measure? Indeed, how can it, under its own guidance, penetrate to a knowledge of the substance of God, when it cannot understand its own? Let us therefore willingly leave to God the knowledge of himself. In the words of Hilary (De Trinit. lib. 1), “He alone is a fit witness to himself, who is known only through himself.” And we will leave this knowledge to God if we conceive of him as he has made himself known, and in our inquiries go to no other source than his word. On this subject we have five homilies of Chrysostom against the Anomoei,#footnote[De Incomprehensit. Dei Natura. (Beveridge’s note.)] in which he tried, but in vain, to check the presumption of the sophists and curb their endless talk. They showed no more modesty here than they usually do in everything else. The very unhappy results of their rashness should warn us to bring more teachableness than cleverness to this question, never to try to search for God anywhere but in his sacred word, and never to speak or think of him further than we have it for our guide. But if the distinction of Father, Son, and Spirit, subsisting in the one Godhead (certainly a subject of great difficulty), gives some minds more trouble and annoyance than is good for them, let us remember that the human mind enters a labyrinth whenever it indulges its curiosity, and so let us submit to be guided by the divine oracles, however far the mystery may be beyond our reach.

22\. It would be tedious, and toilsome to no purpose, to draw up a catalogue of the errors by which the purity of the faith has been attacked on this point of doctrine. Most heretics have made a sweeping assault on the glory of God with their gross ravings, thinking it enough if they could disturb and shake the unwary. From a few individuals numerous sects have sprung up, some of them tearing apart the divine essence, and others confounding the distinction of Persons. But if we hold what has already been shown from Scripture, that the essence of the one God, belonging to the Father, Son, and Spirit, is simple and indivisible, and again, that the Father differs from the Son by some special property, and the Son from the Spirit, the door will be shut against Arius and Sabellius, as well as the other ancient authors of error. But since in our day certain frenzied men have arisen, such as Servetus and others, who have thrown everything into confusion by new tricks, it may be worthwhile briefly to discuss their fallacies.

Servetus so disliked, indeed detested, the name of Trinity that he charged all whom he called Trinitarians with being atheists. I say nothing of the insulting terms he thought fit to use in making his charges. The sum of his speculations was that a threefold Deity is brought in wherever three Persons are said to exist in his essence, and that this Triad was imaginary, since it was inconsistent with the unity of God. At the same time, he claimed that the Persons are certain external ideas which do not truly subsist in the divine essence, but only picture God to us under this or that form; that at first, indeed, there was no distinction in God, because originally the Word was the same as the Spirit, but that ever since Christ came forth God of God, the Spirit also, another God, had proceeded from him. But although he sometimes cloaks his absurdities in allegory, as when he says that the eternal Word of God was the Spirit of Christ with God, and the reflection of the idea, and likewise that the Spirit was a shadow of Deity, in the end he reduces the divinity of both to nothing. He maintains that, according to the mode of distribution, there is a part of God in the Son as well as in the Spirit, just as the same Spirit is substantially a portion of God in us, and also in wood and stone. His absurd babbling about the person of the Mediator will be seen in its own place.#footnote[See Calvin. Defensio Orthodox. Fid. S. Trinit. Adv. Prod. Error. M. Serveti. (Beveridge’s note.)]

The monstrous fiction that a Person is nothing else than a visible appearance of the glory of God needs no long refutation. For when John declares that before the world was created the Logos \[the Word\] was God (John 1:1), he shows that he was something very different from an idea. But if even then, and from the remotest eternity, that Logos who was God was with the Father, and had his own distinct and special glory with the Father (John 17:5), he certainly could not be an external or figurative splendor, but must necessarily have been a hypostasis dwelling inwardly in God himself. And although no mention is made of the Spirit before the account of the creation, he is not brought in there as a shadow, but as the essential power of God, where Moses relates that the shapeless mass was upheld by him (Gen 1:2). It is obvious that the eternal Spirit always existed in God, seeing that he nurtured and sustained the confused materials of heaven and earth before they had order or beauty. Surely he could not then be an image or representation of God, as Servetus dreams. But elsewhere Servetus is forced to disclose his impiety more openly, when he says that God, by his eternal reason decreeing a visible Son for himself, in this way assumed a visible appearance. For if this is true, no other divinity is left to Christ than what is implied in his having been ordained a Son by God’s eternal decree. Moreover, those phantoms which Servetus puts in place of the hypostases he so transforms that he does not hesitate to ascribe new accidental qualities to God. But the most abominable heresy of all is his mixing both the Son and Spirit indiscriminately with all created things. For he openly asserts that there are parts and partitions in the essence of God, and that every such portion is God. This he does especially when he says that the spirits of the faithful are co-eternal and consubstantial with God, although elsewhere he assigns a substantial divinity not only to the soul of man, but to all created things.

23\. This pool has bred another monster not unlike the former. For certain restless spirits, unwilling to share the disgrace and reproach of Servetus’s impiety, have confessed that there are indeed three Persons, but have added, as a reason, that the Father, who alone is truly and properly God, transfused his Divinity into the Son and Spirit when he formed them. Nor do they refrain from such shocking language as this: that the Father is distinguished from the Son and Spirit by this mark, that he is the only #emph[essentiator] \[giver of essence\]. Their first pretext for this is that Christ is regularly called the Son of God. From this they infer that there is no one properly God but the Father. But they forget that, although the name of God is common also to the Son, it is sometimes ascribed to the Father preeminently, as being the source and principle of Divinity; and this is done in order to mark the simple unity of essence. They object that if he is truly the Son of God, it is absurd that he should be considered the Son of a person. I answer that both are true: namely, that he is the Son of God, because he is the Word, begotten of the Father before all ages (for we are not now speaking of the Person of the Mediator); and yet that, for the purpose of explanation, account must be taken of the Person, so that the name God may be understood not in its absolute sense, but as equivalent to Father. For if we hold that there is no other God than the Father, this rank is clearly denied to the Son.

In every case where the Godhead is mentioned, we must by no means admit an antithesis between the Father and the Son, as if the name of God could properly be applied to the Father only. For surely the God who appeared to Isaiah was the one true God, and yet John declares that he was Christ (Isa 6; John 12:41). He who declared by the mouth of Isaiah that he would be “a stone of stumbling” to the Jews was the one God; and yet Paul declares that he was Christ (Isa 8:14; Rom 9:33). He who proclaims through Isaiah, “Every knee will bow before Me,” is the one God; yet Paul again explains that he is Christ (Isa 45:23; Rom 14:11). To this we may add the passages quoted by an Apostle: “O Lord, You laid the foundations of the earth”; “Let all God’s angels worship Him” (Heb 1:10, 6; Ps 102:26; 97:7). All these apply to the one God; and yet the Apostle contends that they are the proper attributes of Christ. There is nothing in the quibble that what properly belongs to God is transferred to Christ because he is the brightness of his glory. Since the name of Jehovah is everywhere applied to Christ, it follows that, in regard to Deity, he is of himself. For if he is Jehovah, it cannot be denied that he is the same God who elsewhere proclaims through Isaiah, “I am the first and I am the last, and there is no God but Me” (Isa 44:6). We would also do well to ponder the words of Jeremiah, “These gods, who have made neither the heavens nor the earth, will perish from this earth and from under these heavens” (Jer 10:11); from which it follows, conversely, that He whose divinity Isaiah repeatedly proves from the creation of the world is none other than the Son of God. And how is it possible that the Creator, who gives being to all, should not be of himself, but should borrow his essence from another? Whoever says that the Son was #emph[essentiated] by the Father#footnote[The French adds, “puisque tels abuseurs forgent des noms contre nature;”—for these perverters coin names against nature. (Beveridge’s note.)] denies his self-existence. Against this, however, the Holy Spirit protests when he calls him Jehovah. If we suppose, then, that the whole essence is in the Father only, the essence either becomes divisible or is denied to the Son, who, being thus robbed of his essence, will be God in title only. If we are to believe these triflers, the divine essence belongs to the Father only, on the ground that he alone has being, and is #emph[essentiator] of the Son. In this way the divinity of the Son will be something abstracted#footnote[The French is, “tiré comme par un álambic;”—drawn off as if through a still. (Beveridge’s note.)] from the essence of God, or the derivation of a part from the whole. On the same principle they must also concede that the Spirit belongs to the Father only. For if the derivation is from the primary essence, which is proper to none but the Father, the Spirit cannot rightly be considered the Spirit of the Son. This view, however, is refuted by the testimony of Paul, when he makes the Spirit common to both Christ and the Father. Moreover, if the Person of the Father is struck out of the Trinity, in what will he differ from the Son and Spirit, except in being the only God? They confess that Christ is God, and that he differs from the Father. If he differs, there must be some mark of distinction between them. Those who place it in the essence plainly reduce the true divinity of Christ to nothing, since divinity cannot exist without essence, and indeed without the entire essence.#footnote[See Bernard, Serm. 80, super Cantica, on the heresy of Gilbert, Bishop of Poictiers \[Poitiers\]. (Beveridge’s note.)] The Father certainly cannot differ from the Son unless he has something that is his own and not common to him and the Son. What, then, do these men point to as the mark of distinction? If it is in the essence, let them tell us whether or not he shared his essence with the Son. This he could not do in part only, for it would be impious to think of a divided God. And besides, on this supposition the divine essence would be torn apart. The whole, entire essence must therefore be common to the Father and the Son; and if so, in respect of essence there is no distinction between them. If they reply that the Father, while essentiating, still remains the only God, being the possessor of the essence, then Christ will be a figurative God, God in name or appearance only and not in reality, because nothing is more distinctive of God than being, according to the words, “I AM has sent me to you#super[+]” (Exod 3:14).

24\. The assumption that whenever God is mentioned absolutely, only the Father is meant, may be proved false by many passages. Even in those which they quote in support of their views they shamefully betray their thoughtlessness, because the name of Son occurs there in contrast, which shows that the name God is used relatively and is in that way confined to the person of the Father. Their objection can be answered in a single word. If the Father alone were not the true God, they say, he would be his own Father. But there is nothing absurd in the name of God being specially applied, in respect of order and degree, to him who not only begot his own wisdom of himself, but is also the God of the Mediator, as I will show more fully in its own place. For ever since Christ was manifested in the flesh he is called the Son of God, not only because, begotten of the Father before all ages, he was the eternal Word, but because he took on the person and office of the Mediator, so that he might unite us to God. Since they are so bold in excluding the Son from the honor of God, I would like to know whether, when he declares that there is “no one good but One, that is, God,” he deprives himself of goodness. I do not speak of his human nature, lest perhaps they should object that whatever goodness was in it came by free gift: I ask whether the eternal Word of God is good, yes or no. If they say no, their impiety is manifest; if yes, they refute themselves. Christ’s seeming, at first glance, to disclaim the name of good (Matt 19:17) rather confirms our view. Since goodness is the special attribute of God alone, and yet was at that time applied to him in the ordinary way of greeting, his rejection of false honor shows that the goodness in which he excels is divine. Again, I ask whether, when Paul affirms that God alone is “immortal,” “wise, and true” (1 Tim 1:17), he reduces Christ to the rank of beings who are mortal, foolish, and false. Is he not immortal, who from the beginning was life, so as to give immortality to the angels? Is he not wise, who is the eternal wisdom of God? Is he not true, who is truth itself?

I ask, moreover, whether they think Christ should be worshiped. If he rightly claims that every knee shall bow to him (Phil 2:10), it follows that he is the God who in the law forbade anyone but himself to be worshiped. If they insist on applying to the Father only the words of Isaiah, “I am, and there is no one besides Me” (Isa 44:6), I turn the passage against them, since we see that everything that is God’s is attributed to Christ.#footnote[The French is expressed somewhat differently, “veu que l’Apostre en l’allegant de Christ, lui attribue tout ce qui est de Dieu;”—seeing that the Apostle, by applying it to Christ, attributes to him everything belonging to God. (Beveridge’s note.)] There is no room for the quibble that Christ was exalted in the flesh in which he humbled himself, and that in respect of the flesh all power is given to him in heaven and on earth. For although the majesty of King and Judge extends to the whole person of the Mediator, yet if he had not been God manifested in the flesh, he could not have been exalted to such a height without God being at odds with himself. Paul settles the dispute admirably when he declares that he was equal with God before he humbled himself and took on the form of a servant (Phil 2:6, 7). Moreover, how could such equality exist, if he were not that God whose name is Jah and Jehovah, who rides upon the cherubim, who is King of all the earth, and King of the ages? Let them protest as they may, Christ cannot be robbed of the honor described by Isaiah, “This, this is our God; we have waited for Him” (Isa 25:9); for these words describe the coming of God the Redeemer, who was not only to bring the people back from the Babylonian captivity, but to restore the Church and make her completely perfect.

Nor does another quibble help them, that Christ was God in his Father. For though we admit that, in respect of order and degree, the beginning of divinity is in the Father, we hold it a detestable fiction to maintain that essence belongs to the Father alone, as if he were the deifier of the Son. On this view either the essence is manifold, or Christ is God only in name and imagination. If they grant that the Son is God, but second to the Father, then the essence which in the Father is unformed and unbegotten will in him be formed and begotten. I know that many who want to be thought wise mock us for drawing the distinction of persons from the words of Moses, where he brings in God as saying, “Let Us make man in Our image” (Gen 1:26). Pious readers, however, see how lamely and absurdly Moses would have introduced this conversation if there were not several persons in the Godhead. It is certain that those whom the Father addresses must have been uncreated. But nothing is uncreated except the one God. Now then, unless they concede that the power of creating and the authority of commanding were common to the Father, Son, and Spirit, it will follow that God did not speak thus inwardly with himself, but addressed other builders outside himself. Finally, a single passage will at once dispose of both these objections. Christ’s declaration that “God is Spirit” (John 4:24) cannot be confined to the Father only, as if the Word were not of a spiritual nature. But if the name Spirit applies to the Son just as to the Father, I infer that the Son is included under the unqualified name of God. He adds immediately after that the only worshipers approved by the Father are those who worship him in spirit and in truth; and from this I also infer that, because Christ performs the office of teacher as one under a head, he applies the name God to the Father, not to destroy his own Divinity, but to raise us up to it, as it were, step by step.

25\. Their delusion lies in dreaming up individuals, each of whom possesses a part of the essence. The Scriptures teach that there is essentially but one God, and therefore that the essence both of the Son and of the Spirit is unbegotten; but inasmuch as the Father is first in order, and of himself begot his own Wisdom, he, as we observed a little earlier, is rightly regarded as the principle and fountain of the whole Godhead. Thus God, taken without qualification, is unbegotten, and the Father, in respect of his person, is unbegotten. For it is absurd to imagine that our doctrine gives any ground for the charge that we set up a quaternity. They falsely and slanderously ascribe to us the invention of their own brains, as if we held that three persons emanate by derivation from one essence,#footnote[The French adds, “Comme trois ruisseaux;”—like three streams. (Beveridge’s note.)] whereas it is plain from our writings that we do not separate the persons from the essence, but make a distinction between the persons residing in it. If the persons were separated from the essence, their argument might have some plausibility; but then there would be a trinity of Gods, not of persons comprehended in one God. This answers their futile question whether or not the essence joins in forming the Trinity—as if we imagined that three Gods were derived from it. Their objection that there would then be a Trinity without God springs from the same absurdity. Although the essence does not contribute to the distinction as if it were a part or member, the persons are not without it or outside it; for the Father, if he were not God, could not be the Father; nor could the Son be Son unless he were God. We say, then, that the Godhead is absolutely of itself. And from this we also hold that the Son, regarded as God, and without reference to person, is also of himself; though we also say that, regarded as Son, he is of the Father. Thus his essence is without beginning, while his person has its beginning in God. And indeed, the orthodox writers who in former times spoke of the Trinity used this term only with reference to the Persons. To have included the essence in the distinction would have been not only an absurd error but gross impiety. For those who class the three thus—Essence, Son, and Spirit#footnote[The French adds, “Comme si l’essense étoit au lieu de la personne du Pére;”—as if the essence were in place of the person of the Father. (Beveridge’s note.)]—plainly do away with the essence of the Son and Spirit; otherwise the parts, being intermingled, would merge into each other—a fault in any distinction.#footnote[The French is somewhat differently expressed: “Car le Fils a quelque l’estre, ou il n’en a point. S’il en a, voila deux essences pour jouster l’un contre autre; s’il n’en a point, ce ne seroit qu’une ombre.” For the Son has some being, or he has none. If some, here are two essences to clash with each other; if none, he is only a shadow. (Beveridge’s note.)] In short, if God and Father were synonymous terms, the Father would be deifier in a sense that would leave the Son nothing but a shadow; and the Trinity would be nothing more than the union of one God with two created things.

26\. To the objection that if Christ is properly God, he is improperly called the Son of God, the answer has already been given: when one person is compared with another, the name God is not used without qualification, but is restricted to the Father, regarded as the beginning of the Godhead—not by #emph[essentiating], as fanatics absurdly put it, but in respect of order. This is the sense in which we are to understand the words Christ addressed to the Father: “This is eternal life, that they may know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom You have sent” (John 17:3). For, speaking in the person of the Mediator, he holds a middle place between God and man; yet in such a way that his majesty is not diminished by it. For though he humbled (emptied) himself, he did not lose the glory which he had with the Father, though it was hidden from the world. So in the Epistle to the Hebrews (Heb 1:10; 2:9), though the apostle confesses that Christ was made a little lower than the angels, he does not hesitate at the same time to assert that he is the eternal God who founded the earth. We must hold, therefore, that as often as Christ, in the character of Mediator, addresses the Father, he includes his own divinity also under the term God. Thus, when he says to the apostles, “It is for your#super[+] benefit that I am going away,” “The Father is greater than I” (John 16:7; 14:28), he does not attribute to himself merely a secondary divinity, as if in regard to eternal essence he were inferior to the Father; but, having obtained heavenly glory, he gathers the faithful together to share it with him. He places the Father in the higher degree, inasmuch as the full perfection of brightness seen in heaven differs from that measure of glory which he himself displayed when clothed in flesh. For the same reason Paul says that Christ will give back “the kingdom to God the Father,” “so that God may be all in all” (1 Cor 15:24, 28). Nothing can be more absurd than to deny that Christ’s divinity is everlasting. But if he will never cease to be the Son of God, but will always remain the same as he was from the beginning, it follows that under the name of Father the one divine essence common to both is comprehended. And surely Christ came down to us for the very purpose of raising us to the Father, and in doing so, at the same time, raising us to himself, inasmuch as he is one with the Father. It is therefore wrong and impious to confine the name of God to the Father so as to deny it to the Son. For on this very account John declares that he is the true God (1 John 5:20), so that no one would think he stood beneath the Father in a second rank of divinity. I wonder what these makers of new gods mean, when they confess that Christ is truly God, and yet exclude him from the godhead of the Father—as if there could be any true God but the one God, or as if transfused divinity were not a mere modern fiction.

27\. As for the many passages they gather from Irenaeus, where he maintains that the Father of Christ is the only eternal God of Israel, they betray shameful ignorance or very great dishonesty. For they ought to have observed that this holy man was contending against certain frenzied people who denied that the Father of Christ was the God who had spoken of old by Moses and the prophets, and held that he was some phantom or other produced from the pollution of the world. His whole aim, therefore, is to make it plain that in the Scriptures no other God is proclaimed but the Father of Christ, and that it is wicked to imagine any other. So there is nothing strange in his so often concluding that the God of Israel was no other than he who is celebrated by Christ and the apostles. Now, when a different heresy has to be resisted, we also say with truth that the God who appeared to the fathers of old was no other than Christ. Moreover, if it is objected that he was the Father, we have the answer ready: while we contend for the divinity of the Son, we by no means exclude the Father. If readers pay attention to Irenaeus’s purpose, the dispute is over. Indeed, we have only to look at lib. 3 c. 6, where the pious writer insists on this one point, “that he who in Scripture is called God absolutely and without qualification is truly the only God; and that Christ is called God absolutely.” Let us remember (as appears from the whole work, and especially from lib. 2 c. 46) that the point under discussion was that the name of Father is not applied in riddles and parables to one who was not truly God. We may add that in lib. 3 c. 9 he contends that the Son as well as the Father, jointly, was the God proclaimed by the prophets and apostles. He afterwards explains (lib. 3 c. 12) how Christ, who is Lord of all, and King and Judge, received power from him who is God of all—namely, in respect of the subjection in which he humbled himself, even to the death of the cross. At the same time, a little later he affirms (lib. 3 c. 16) that the Son is the maker of heaven and earth, who gave the law by the hand of Moses and appeared to the fathers. If any babbler now insists that, according to Irenaeus, the Father alone is the God of Israel, I will refer him to a passage in which Irenaeus distinctly says (lib. 3 c. 18, 23) that Christ is ever one and the same, and also applies to Christ the words of the prophecy of Habakkuk, “God comes from the south.” To the same effect he says (lib. 4 c. 9), “Therefore Christ himself, with the Father, is the God of the living.” And in the 12th chapter of the same book he explains that Abraham believed God because Christ is the maker of heaven and earth, and the only God.

28\. They have no better ground for claiming Tertullian as a patron. Though his style is sometimes rough and obscure, he sets out the doctrine we maintain without any ambiguity: namely, that while there is one God, his Word nevertheless exists by dispensation or economy; that there is only one God in unity of substance, but that nevertheless, by the mystery of dispensation, the unity is arranged into Trinity; that there are three, not in state but in degree, not in substance but in form, not in power but in order.#footnote[Tertullianus, lib. adv. Praxeam;—Perversitas hæc (Praxeæ scil.) se existimat meram veritatem possidere, dum unicum Deum non alias putat credendum, quam si ipsum eundemque et Patrem et Filium et Spiritum sanctum dicat: quasi non sic quoque unus sit omnia, dum ex uno omnia, per substantiæ scilicet unitatem, et nihilominus custodiatur οἰκονομίας sacramentum, quæ unitatem in trinitatem disponit, tres dirigens, Patrem, Filium, et Spiritum sanctum. Tres autem non statu, sed gradu: nec substantia, sed forma: nec potestate, sed specie: unius autem substantiæ, et unius status, et unius potestatis: quia unus Deus, ex quo et gradus isti, formæ et species, in nomine Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus sancti deputantur. Quomodo numerum sine divisione patiuntur, procedentes tractatus demonstrabunt, &c. \[This perversity (that is, of Praxeas) thinks it holds the pure truth, while it supposes that the one God can be believed in no other way than by calling the very same one the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit: as if one were not all in this way too, since all are from one, namely by the unity of substance, while the mystery of the economy is nonetheless kept, which arranges the unity into a trinity, setting in order three, the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Yet three, not in state but in degree; not in substance but in form; not in power but in aspect; but of one substance, one state and one power, because there is one God, from whom these degrees, forms and aspects are reckoned under the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. How they admit number without division, the treatise as it proceeds will show, etc.\] (Beveridge’s note.)] He says indeed that he holds the Son to be second to the Father; but he means that the only difference is one of distinction. In one place he says the Son is visible; but after he has reasoned on both sides, he declares that he is invisible regarded as the Word. Finally, by affirming that the Father is marked out by his own Person, he shows that he is very far from supporting the fiction which we are refuting. And although he does not acknowledge any other God than the Father, yet, explaining himself in the immediate context, he shows that he does not speak exclusively with respect to the Son, because he denies that the Son is a different God from the Father; and so, that the one supremacy is not violated by the distinction of Person. And it is easy to gather his meaning from the whole tenor of his argument. For he contends against Praxeas that, although God has three distinct Persons, yet there are not several gods, nor is the unity divided. According to Praxeas’s fiction, Christ could not be God without being the Father also; and this is why Tertullian labors so much on the distinction. When he calls the Word and Spirit a portion of the whole, the expression, though harsh, may be excused, since it does not refer to the substance, but only (as Tertullian himself testifies) denotes the arrangement and economy which belongs to the persons only. So he asks, “How many persons, Praxeas, do you think there are, but just as many as there are names?” In the same way, a little later he says, “That they may believe the Father and the Son, each in his own name and person.” This, I think, is enough to refute the brazenness of those who try to deceive the simple by appealing to the authority of Tertullian.

29\. Surely, whoever compares the writings of the ancient Fathers with one another will not find anything in Irenaeus different from what is taught by those who came after him. Justin is one of the most ancient, and he agrees with us in everything. Let them object that by him and by others the Father of Christ is called the one God. The same thing is taught by Hilary, who uses the still harsher expression that Eternity is in the Father. Is it so that he may withhold the divine essence from the Son? His whole work is a defense of the doctrine which we maintain; and yet these men are not ashamed to pick out some mutilated excerpts or other to persuade us that Hilary is a patron of their heresy. As for what they allege from Ignatius, if they would have it count for anything, let them prove that the apostles made laws about Lent and other corruptions. Nothing could be more nauseating than the absurdities which have been published under the name of Ignatius; and so the conduct of those who equip themselves with such masks to deceive is all the less to be tolerated.

Moreover, the agreement of the ancient Fathers clearly appears from this: at the Council of Nice \[Nicaea\], Arius did not attempt to cloak his heresy with the authority of any approved author; and no Greek or Latin writer excuses himself for dissenting from his predecessors. There is no need to observe how carefully Augustine, to whom all these scoundrels are most violently opposed, examined all the ancient writings, and how reverently he embraced the doctrine taught by them.#footnote[August. lib. de Trinit. &c. (Beveridge’s note.)] He is most careful to state the grounds on which he is forced to differ from them, even in the smallest point. On this subject too, if he finds anything ambiguous or obscure in other writers, he does not hide it.#footnote[Athanasius puts it thus, learnedly and piously:—“On this subject, though you cannot explain yourself, you are not for that reason to distrust the Holy Scriptures. It is better, while hesitating through ignorance, to be silent and believe, than not to believe because you hesitate.” (Beveridge’s note.)] And he takes it as an acknowledged fact that the doctrine which these men oppose was received without dispute from the earliest antiquity. At the same time, he was not ignorant of what some others had taught before him. This is obvious from a single expression. When he says (De Doct. Christ. lib. 1) that “unity is in the Father,” will they claim that he then forgot himself? In another passage he clears himself of every such charge, when he calls the Father the beginning of the Godhead, as being from none—thus wisely inferring that the name of God is specially ascribed to the Father because, unless the beginning were from him, the simple unity of essence could not be maintained. I hope the pious reader will grant that I have now disposed of all the slanders by which Satan has so far tried to pervert or obscure the pure doctrine of faith. The whole substance of the doctrine has, I trust, been faithfully set out, if my readers will set bounds to their curiosity, and not crave perplexing disputes more eagerly than they ought. I did not undertake to satisfy those who delight in intemperate speculation; but I have not deliberately left out anything which I thought told against me. At the same time, seeking the edification of the Church, I have thought it better not to touch on various topics which could have yielded little profit, while they would have needlessly burdened and wearied the reader. For instance, what is the use of discussing#footnote[As Lombard does at length, lib. 1 dist. 9. (Beveridge’s note.)] whether or not the Father is always begetting? This idea of continual generation becomes an absurd fiction the moment it is seen that from eternity there were three persons in one God.
